// The checked build -- `-fcheck`, and what `-O0` already means.
//
// `memory.md` states one obligation per access: aligned, inside an object, and --
// the half a compiler can *check* rather than define -- against an address that is
// not null and an index that is inside the extent the type or the descriptor gives.
// `-O0` is the build that verifies it, and `-fcheck` asks for it at any level.
//
// The guards are three, and each is a test the compiler writes in front of the
// access it belongs to:
//
//   * `memory-null`          the address against zero
//   * `memory-misaligned`    the address's low bits against the type's alignment
//   * `memory-out-of-bounds` the index against the count (`a[i]` on an array) or
//                            against the view's own length (`s[i]` on a slice)
//
// Nothing in this program traps, which is the point of the example: a correct
// program pays for the checks and answers the same numbers it would have answered
// without them. The one thing to take away is *when* a trap happens -- at the
// access, with the site in the message -- rather than somewhere later, in code
// that did nothing wrong.
//
//   $ mincc run examples/018_checked.mx
//   total 24 from 4 elements, middle 12, again 24
//   last 9 (the view is 4 long, and the language says so)
//
// The same file with the guard off (`mincc run -fno-check`) prints the same thing,
// because the accesses are inside their objects. An access that is *not* is the
// case the build exists for -- and `past` below is one, uncalled here so that this
// program stays a correct one. A program that calls a subscript out of range stops
// with the site in the message:
//
//   mincc: trap: memory-out-of-bounds at <file>:<line>:<col>
//   mincc: error: the program was terminated abnormally
//
// which is the difference between a bug that is found where it was written and one
// that is found later, in code that did nothing wrong: with the flag off, the same
// access reads whatever is past the array.
//
// Three details of the design are visible from here. The index comparison is
// **unsigned**, so a negative index is caught by the same instruction as an index
// past the end. The extent of `a[i]` is a number in its *type* and the extent of
// `s[i]` is the descriptor's `len` **word**, which is why the two are different
// rows of the same guard. And a subscript through a *pointer* (`p[i]`) has no
// extent to compare against -- the object `p` names is not in this unit -- so it is
// guarded against null and alignment and not against its extent, which is the
// honest answer rather than a guess (`checks.md`).

extern fn i32 printf(format: str, ...);

// Two guards on one access: the address against null, and the index against the
// view's length. Both are the record's, and neither is proved away -- a checker
// that deletes the checks it can prove stops reporting the day the proof has a bug.
fn i32 at(view: []i32, i: i32) {
  return view[i];
}

// `a[i]` on a named array: the count is four, in the type, and the guard compares
// against exactly that.
fn i32 sum4(table: [4]i32) {
  let total: i32 = 0;
  for let i: i32 = 0; i < 4; i += 1 {
    total = total + table[i];
  }
  return total;
}

// Nine elements into a view of whatever length it is handed. The guard for this
// one is emitted like every other; nothing calls it from this file, which is what
// makes the program above a correct one *and* leaves the reader a site to point
// `mincc run` at.
fn i32 past(view: []i32) {
  return at(view, 9);
}

fn i32 main() {
  let table: [4]i32 = [3, 5, 7, 9];
  let view: []i32 = table[..];
  let total: i32 = sum4(table);
  let middle: i32 = at(view, 1) + at(view, 2);
  let again: i32 = 0;
  for let i: i32 = 0; i < 4; i += 1 {
    again = again + at(view, i);
  }
  printf("total %d from 4 elements, middle %d, again %d\n", total, middle, again);
  printf("last %d (the view is 4 long, and the language says so)\n", at(view, 3));
  return 0;
}
